{
  "summary": "The round-11 synthesis is the most implementation-ready proposal yet but has five unresolved issues that would cause problems during implementation or runtime. (1) The dependency_events field clearing on plan approval is destructive in multi-dependency scenarios: if feature C depends on both A and B, and A is rejected triggering re-planning, the dependency_events are cleared when C's new plan is approved — but if B is subsequently rejected, C's second re-planning cycle has no record that A was also rejected, and the planner may reintroduce assumptions about A. The clearing should be selective (mark individual events as 'addressed' rather than bulk-clearing) or dependency_events should be treated as an append-only audit trail that the planning prompt filters by recency, not by presence. (2) The 'advisory for humans, mandatory for auto-mode' split creates a UX contradiction: the WebUI renders a blocked badge on a feature card, signaling 'this cannot proceed,' but manual moves silently bypass it with only a log warning. Users will either (a) learn to ignore the badge, making the dependency UI visual noise, or (b) be confused when auto-mode refuses to process features they manually moved past the block. The design needs a visible 'dependency override' state — when a user manually moves a blocked feature into impl_stages, the blocked badge should change to an 'override' indicator (e.g., yellow warning icon instead of red lock), and the auto-run skip logic should respect manual overrides by checking whether the feature was manually moved into its current stage (detectable from history entries with 'Moved via server command'). (3) The auto-run feature selection does not account for dependency ordering. Currently _auto_run_check() iterates features in stage-priority order (implementing > review > ideating > approved) and picks the first non-skipped candidate. But if features B and C both depend on A, and all three are in 'approved', auto-mode might pick B first, find it blocked, skip it, pick C, find it blocked, skip it, then never reach A because it appears later in the iteration — or waste cycles skipping blocked features every 10 seconds before eventually picking A. The impl_stages pass should apply a topological sort to candidates, preferring features with no unresolved dependencies (or whose dependencies are all resolved), ensuring that dependency-free features are processed first. This is a simple sort on len(unresolved_deps) applied to the candidate list before iteration. (4) The resolve_dependencies() function signature taking (id, stage, depends_on) tuples forces every caller to destructure FeatureFile objects into tuples before calling — this is boilerplate that will be duplicated across server.py, pipeline.py, and any future caller. Since state.py already defines FeatureFile, the function should accept List[FeatureFile] directly and extract what it needs internally. The tuple-based interface only makes sense if callers don't have FeatureFile objects, but both identified callers (DependencyGraph in server.py and CLI pipeline.py) always have loaded FeatureFile lists from list_all(). (5) The design doesn't address what happens when a dangling dependency reference exists — a depends_on ID that maps to no existing feature (e.g., the feature file was manually deleted from disk, or the ID was mistyped during a manual JSON edit). The resolve_dependencies() function and is_blocked() logic must explicitly handle this case: should a dangling reference be treated as blocking (safe but potentially deadlocking — the feature can never unblock without manual intervention) or non-blocking (permissive but silently ignoring data corruption)? The correct answer is to treat it as blocking AND surface it as a distinct error state ('dangling dependency' vs 'unresolved dependency') in both the broadcast blocked-status and the auto-run skip log, so users can identify and fix the broken reference. The detect_cycle() function also needs to handle dangling refs — a cycle check that follows a dangling edge will either crash (KeyError on the adjacency list) or silently ignore it, neither of which is correct."
}
